<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext policy.

  WHY: the backend-hosting decision is remote/self-hosted (ADR-005), and a
  self-hosted API is routinely plain http:// on a LAN (127.0.0.1:8000,
  192.168.1.20:8000). Android blocks cleartext from API 28+, which would break
  the Settings "Test" probe and every API call for those setups.

  DESIGN TENSION (documented, not hidden):
  Android's <network-security-config> <domain> element accepts literal host
  names or IPs only — it does NOT accept CIDR ranges, so you cannot whitelist
  "the whole private LAN" statically. Because the API base is a *runtime,
  user-entered* value (ADR-005), any static allowlist is either too narrow
  (breaks real setups) or too broad.

  DECISION: permit cleartext globally here. This is acceptable for this client
  because:
    - The backend has NO auth and stores no user data/credentials, so there is
      nothing secret for a MITM to steal.
    - The risk is limited to an attacker on the same LAN swapping a stream
      URL, which is already an accepted exposure for a self-hosted media proxy.
    - Production CDN URLs are https regardless; this config only governs the
      API host the user chose.

  HARDENING OPTIONS if this ever ships publicly (pick one, don't ship both):
    a) Require https in the backend and set cleartextTrafficPermitted=false.
    b) Ship a curated <domain-config> list of known self-hosted hostnames.
  See docs/Risks and Compliance.md § Security.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
